fficulty of hard to automate  liquid liquid separations we have used  the captiva emr lipid plate to complete  a solid phase  separation of proteins metabolites and  lipids  as shown in this diagram the captiva emr  plate contains a solid sorbent  that specifically binds lipids  containing acyl hydrocarbon chains  and polar head groups this orbit does  not have an affinity  for metabolites that do not have  hydrocarbon chains  we initially prepare the sample by  adding an organic solvent to form a  protein precipitate  then when we add the sample to the  captiva emr lipid plate the protein  precipitate is caught on a filter above  the emr  sorbent the polar metabolites flow  through the sorbent  and the soluble lipids are captured on  the sorbent  thus the polar metabolites are collected  in the plate flow through  and can be dried and reconstituted prior  to lcms analysis  the lipids are then eluded from the  captiva emr plate using  a relatively hydrophobic aleutian  solvent  thus metabolites and lipids are  separated and collected from each  individual sample  during the development of this workflow  some optimization was done to increase  metabolite recovery  it was found that increasing the water  content from 18  to 50 percent for a mixture of  metabolite standards  prior to passing them through the  captiva emr lipid plate  could increase the fraction of  metabolite standards that made it  through the plate  improvements in recoveries were  particularly good for bulkier  amphiphilic  and more hydrophobic molecules when  increasing the water content of the  samples  we wanted to maintain protein  precipitation as much as possible  to accomplish this proteins are first  precipitated by an organic solvent  then the water is added second to a  final content of 50 percent  this provides significantly larger  amounts of precipitate  than when the organic solvent and water  are added together  thus maintaining a protein precipitate  level similar  to that obtained by adding an organic  solvent alone  you may now be wondering what the  metabolite recoveries look like for this  workflow  when the cell sample matrix is present  to test this we took frozen cell pellets  each containing 1 million cells and  prepared them using our dual metabolite  plus lipid workflow  then to monitor recoveries we added a 13  labeled metabolite mixture  to the samples before or after  they were passed through the captiva emr  lipid plate  the percent recovery for each carbon 13  labeled metabolite was measured  by dividing the metabolite peak area  from the pre-spike sample  by the peak area from the post-spike  sample  for this experiment there were six  pre-spiked  and six post bike samples and we  compared the dual  metabolite plus lipid workflow to a  liquid extraction workflow  samples were analyzed by lcms to find  the carbon 13 labeled metabolite peaks  here are the plotted recoveries for the  carbon 13 labeled metabolites  monitored with this experiment on the  left are results  from the dual metabolite plus lipid  workflow  and on the right are results from the  liquid extraction workflow  overall the dual workflow provides  excellent recoveries  and also has a lower variability or a  lower  average standard deviation than the  liquid extraction workflow  thus although the dual workflow can  provide access to both metabolite and  lipid fractions  it can also be used to obtain great and  consistent recoveries for investigations  focused on polar metabolites  next we looked at the lipid extraction  performance for the dual  metabolite plus lipid workflow in this  case we had six  sample replicates for both the dual  workflow and a liquid liquid extraction  workflow  lipids were extracted using both  workflows and dried  and reconstituted for lcms analysis  the data files were then analyzed using  lipid annotator  profinder and mass profiler professional  to find the annotated lipids extracted  by each workflow  this is a heat map from mass p